arXiv:2509.15861v1 [cs.LG] 19 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Van-Tuan Tran
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computer Science and Statistics, Trinity College Dublin, Ireland
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hong-Hanh Nguyen-Le
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computer Science, University College Dublin, Ireland
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Quoc-Viet Pham
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding Author. Email: viet.pham@tcd.ie</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Computer Science and Statistics, Trinity College Dublin, Ireland
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural networks unintentionally memorize training data, creating privacy risks in federated learning (FL) systems, such as inference and reconstruction attacks on sensitive data. To mitigate these risks and to comply with privacy regulations, Federated Unlearning (FU) has been introduced to enable participants in FL systems to remove their data’s influence from the global model. However, current FU methods primarily act post-hoc, struggling to efficiently erase information deeply memorized by neural networks. We argue that effective unlearning necessitates a paradigm shift: designing FL systems inherently amenable to forgetting. To this end, we propose a learning-to-unlearn Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning (ToFU) framework that incorporates transformations during the learning process to reduce memorization of specific instances. Our theoretical analysis reveals how transformation composition provably bounds instance-specific information, directly simplifying subsequent unlearning. Crucially, ToFU can work as a plug-and-play framework that improves the performance of existing FU methods. Experiments on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and the MUFAC benchmark show that ToFU outperforms existing FU baselines, enhances performance when integrated with current methods, and reduces unlearning time.</p>
  
</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_paperid"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">paperid: </span>5087</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neural networks have been demonstrated to unintentionally memorize their training data in both centralized settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and decentralized settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. This memorization capability, however, poses significant privacy risks as it potentially enables the extraction of sensitive training data through various inference attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and model inversion attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. These privacy concerns are particularly pronounced in federated learning (FL) systems, where models are trained across distributed client devices containing personal data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. As a result, there is a growing need for mechanisms that allow the removal of private samples from trained models. This is also a requirement that is increasingly formalized in regulatory frameworks such as GDPR<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          https://gdpr-info.eu/art-17-gdpr/</span></span></span> and CCPA<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          https://oag.ca.gov/privacy/ccpa</span></span></span>.
In response, federated unlearning (FU) has been developed as a specialized approach, enabling participants to eradicate their data’s influence from collaboratively trained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. However, FU presents unique challenges compared to centralized unlearning: data remains distributed across clients, communication bandwidth is limited, the server cannot directly access client data, and client knowledge permeates throughout the global model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite significant progress, current FU approaches focus on modifying the model architectures or parameters to achieve unlearning objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halimi et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These methods often overlook the fundamental relationship between learning and unlearning processes, particularly how the memorization property of neural networks impacts unlearning effectiveness. Current approaches treat the unlearning challenge as separate from the learning process, failing to address how the initial training methodology affects subsequent unlearning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We hypothesize that improving unlearning performance requires reducing neural networks’ tendency to memorize specific training instances. Instead of allowing models to memorize individual data points, we aim to guide them toward learning only task-relevant features during the training process. Note that, to enable effective unlearning in FL settings, it is crucial the reduce the permeation of knowledge during the collaborative training process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.
To do that, we propose incorporating a sequence of transformations applied to local data samples during training, compelling the model to learn transformation-invariant features rather than instance-specific features.
This strategy reduces explicit memorization of training data and consequently enhances unlearning performance when removal requests are processed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The analysis on the influence of transformations on unlearning performance leads us to propose a learning-to-unlearn <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ransf<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o</span>rmation-guided <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ederated <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">U</span>nlearning (ToFU) framework. ToFU integrates transformations into the federated learning process to simplify the subsequent unlearning process while preserving model utility. By establishing foundations for effective unlearning during the initial training phase, ToFU represents a paradigm shift from treating unlearning as a post-hoc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halimi et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> to designing learning systems with unlearning capabilities built in from the outset.
In particular, our framework offers key advantages: (1) <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduced Memorization</span>: ToFU enforces the models to learn transformation-invariant features, substantially reducing the model’s ability to memorize sample-specific information;
(2) <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Seamless Versatility</span>: ToFU is designed to work as plug-and-play framework that improves the performance of existing FU methods;
(3) <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduced Unlearning Time</span>: By incorporating inherent unlearning capabilities into the FL learning process, ToFU simplifies the unlearning process to a lightweight fine-tuning operation, significantly reducing computational overhead and waiting time for non-unlearning clients.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15861v1/progressive_transform_illustration_CIFAR100.png" id="S1.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/30;" width="215" height="30" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Increasing intensity level of transformation applied to forgetting samples.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15861v1/progressive_masking_illustration_CIFAR100.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/30;" width="215" height="30" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Increasing masking ratio applied to forgetting samples.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15861v1/progressive_transform_CIFAR100.svg" id="S1.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/145;" width="215" height="145"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Overall unlearning performance improves when increasing the intensity level of transformation on the forgetting samples.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15861v1/progressive_masking_CIFAR100.svg" id="S1.F1.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/147;" width="215" height="147"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span>Overall unlearning performance improves when increasing the masking ratio on the forgetting samples.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Experiments on CIFAR-100 to show that the unlearning process becomes more effective when models learn from transformed samples. (Left) Progressive Transformation and (Right) Progressive Masking. The top figures present forgetting samples with increasing intensity level, while the bottom figures illustrate the correlation between unlearning performance and the intensity level of transformation. <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is the root mean squared linear regression error, <math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> are the Spearman’s rank correlation and Pearson’s correlation coefficients, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contribution.</span> The main contributions are summarized as
follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide theoretical and empirical results regarding the unlearning performance under a sequence of transformations of training samples. Our analysis reveals a novel insight: the unlearning process becomes more effective when models learn from transformed samples rather than memorizing raw data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a learning-to-unlearn Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning (ToFU) framework that dynamically applies multiple transformations to training samples during the federated learning process. This novel approach forces the global model to learn invariant features that remain effective across all transformations of the same sample.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments across multiple datasets, including CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and the MUFAC unlearning benchmark, to demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. The results show that ToFU not only outperforms existing FU baselines but can also be integrated with current methods to enhance their performance significantly.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Forget and Retain sets)
Given a dataset <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\mathcal{Z}^{(i)}=(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\mathcal{Z}^{(i)}=(x^{(i)},y^{(i)})\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the model corresponding to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional real vector subspace. The forget, retain sets according to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}}\subset\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">retain</mtext></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}}\subset\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the dataset determined by <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}=\{\mathcal{Z}=(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}:f_{\theta^{\prime}}(\mathcal{Z})=False\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}=\{\mathcal{Z}=(x,y)\in\mathcal{D}:f_{\theta^{\prime}}(\mathcal{Z})=False\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">retain</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}}=\mathcal{D}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the local model parameterized by <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> that means <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\mathcal{Z}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}},f_{\theta}(\mathcal{Z})=True" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">retain</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\mathcal{Z}\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{retain}},f_{\theta}(\mathcal{Z})=True</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Machine Unlearning)
An unlearning process <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(f_{\theta},\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(f_{\theta},\mathcal{D},\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a transformation from a trained model <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, on the dataset <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, to a unlearned model <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> which eliminates any influence of the forget set <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{forget}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>’s behavior.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Federated Unlearning)
Let <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a set of local models trained on local datasets <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{D}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{D}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> distributed among <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> clients in a federated learning system, and let <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">global</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the global aggregate model. For any client <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in K</annotation></semantics></math> with a subset <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}\subset\mathcal{D}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}\subset\mathcal{D}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> designated for removal, a federated unlearning process <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{FU}(f_{\text{global}},\{f_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\mathcal{D}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}\}_{k=1}^{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">global</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{FU}(f_{\text{global}},\{f_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\mathcal{D}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}\}_{k=1}^{K})</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a transformation from a globally trained model <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{global}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">global</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{global}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a unlearned model <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{global}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">global</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{global}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which performs as if the global model had been trained with the federated learning protocol where client <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> had only used <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{retain}}=\mathcal{D}_{k}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">retain</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">forget</mtext></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{retain}}=\mathcal{D}_{k}\setminus\mathcal{D}_{k,\text{forget}}</annotation></semantics></math> during training, while maintaining the privacy constraints of the federated learning framework.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish the theoretical foundations of transformation-guided unlearning and empirically demonstrate the correlation between transformation intensity and unlearning effectiveness. We first formulate the unlearning problem through the lens of mutual information (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Unlearning Optimization Problem ‣ 3 Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), then develop a theoretical framework showing that ”<span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">increasing the intensity level of transformation applied to forgetting samples results in better unlearning performance</span>” (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Transformation Composition for Improving Unlearning Performance ‣ 3 Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and finally validate our theoretical insights through experiments on benchmark datasets (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Empirical Validation of Transformation Effects ‣ 3 Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Unlearning Optimization Problem</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Mutual Information)
For any model parameterized by <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> trained on dataset <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we can determine the predictive distribution <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> for input <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Given two models with parameters <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the mutual information between these models with respect to a dataset <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined using the Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{S})=D_{KL}(p_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}(x,y)\|p_{\theta}(x|y)p_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{S})=D_{KL}(p_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}(x,y)\|p_{\theta}(x|y)p_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta,\theta^{\prime}}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the joint distribution of outputs from both models, and <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta^{\prime}}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> represent their respective marginal distributions over dataset <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, mutual information measures how much information is shared between models. When <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{S})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{S})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the models are informationally independent with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> has effectively <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unlearned</span> the patterns in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> that were captured by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Based on this measure, we formulate the unlearning challenge as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unlearning Optimization Problem.</span> Given a dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, a forget set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{forget}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{forget}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as a trained model on <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, find <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})=0\text{ and }I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain})\;\text{is maximized}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>is maximized</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})=0\text{ and }I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain})\;\text{is maximized}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The ideal unlearning process has two goals: (1) the unlearned model should behave as if it never saw the forgotten data, and (2) it should preserve all knowledge from the retained data. Since perfectly achieving both goals simultaneously is often impossible in practice, we relax it to:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})\text{ and }\max I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})\text{ and }\max I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which leads to the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}=\min_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}\frac{I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})}{I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}=\min_{\theta^{\prime}\in\Theta}\frac{I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{forget})}{I(\theta,\theta^{\prime};D_{retain})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While existing approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Halimi et al., 2022</a>)</cite> assume that all forgetting samples exert equal influence on the unlearning process, we argue that applying different levels of transformations to forgetting samples can significantly improve unlearning performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Transformation Composition for Improving Unlearning Performance</h3>

<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Transformation)
Let <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}:\Theta\rightarrow\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}:\Theta\rightarrow\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> be a one-shot transformation, <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,1,2,\dots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,1,2,\dots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, defined on parameter space <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer. A m-intensity transformation on <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is a function composed of k one-shot transformations: <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{(m)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\dots\circ\mathcal{T}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{(m)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\dots\circ\mathcal{T}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the composition operator.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now establish how composing multiple transformations enhances unlearning effectiveness:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Transformation Composition for Unlearning)
Given <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{0}\equiv\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{0}\equiv\theta</annotation></semantics></math> as a parameterized model of dataset <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and two simple transformations <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1}\equiv\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1}\equiv\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{2}\equiv\theta^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{2}\equiv\theta^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which aim to unlearn the forget sets <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{forget}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{forget}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{forget}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{forget}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)}=\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\mathcal{T}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)}=\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\mathcal{T}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item ltx_centering" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{1};D_{forget})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{1};D_{forget})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item ltx_centering" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{2};D_{forget})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{2};D_{forget})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item ltx_centering" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{2};D_{forget})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{2};D_{forget})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item ltx_centering" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{1};D_{forget})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{\prime\prime(2)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}_{1};D_{forget})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{forget}=D_{forget}^{\prime}\cup D_{forget}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{forget}=D_{forget}^{\prime}\cup D_{forget}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Transformation Composition for Improving Unlearning Performance ‣ 3 Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Proof of Theorem on Transformation Composition for Unlearning ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>) demonstrates a key insight: applying two transformations in sequence progressively on a specific sample reduces the mutual information of the model about that sample. As a result, it helps reduce the model’s ability to remember specific data points, enabling the subsequent unlearning process. Each additional transformation further weakens the connection between the model and the forgotten data, regardless of the order in which transformations are applied.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Monotonic Unlearning Performance Improvement)
For any sequence of transformations <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{1},\mathcal{T}_{2},\dots,\mathcal{T}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{1},\mathcal{T}_{2},\dots,\mathcal{T}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> designed to unlearn different aspects of <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{forget}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{forget}</annotation></semantics></math>, and any composition <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{(m)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\dots\circ\mathcal{T}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{(m)}=\mathcal{T}_{1}\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\dots\circ\mathcal{T}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{(m)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{(m-1)};D_{forget})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{(m)};D_{forget})\leq I(\theta,\mathcal{T}^{(m-1)};D_{forget})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This corollary (Proof in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Proof of Corollary on Monotonic Unlearning Performance ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) follows directly from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3.2 Transformation Composition for Improving Unlearning Performance ‣ 3 Theoretical and Empirical Analysis: Unlearning from the Lens of Transformation ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and extends to multiple transformations. It shows that increasing the intensity level of transformation monotonically improves the unlearning performance with respect to the forget set, establishing a theoretical foundation for our transformation-guided approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Empirical Validation of Transformation Effects</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our theoretical findings, we conduct experiments on the CIFAR-100 dataset by applying two different approaches: (1) consecutive augmentations and (2) increasing levels of masking ratio using the Cutout transformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeVries and Taylor (2017)</a></cite>. More details about applied transformations are provided in Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (Appendix). Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows a positive correlation between the intensity level of transformation and the unlearning performance, which implies that increasing transformation intensity improves the unlearning performance. We also provide additional experiments on CIFAR-10 in the Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B More Experiments on CIFAR-10 ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> that further demonstrate the role of transformation in improving unlearning performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the plug-and-play Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning (ToFU) framework that simplifies the unlearning process and improves the unlearning performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15861v1/val_learning_curve_CIFAR-10-100.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/246;" width="405" height="246"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Learning curves of validation accuracy on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 datasets. ToFU achieves better validation accuracy and faster convergence compared to the naive strategy of stacking a fixed number of transformations for every training sample.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key Idea.</span> The fundamental principle of ToFU is that reducing memorization of specific training instances facilitates more effective unlearning. To do that, multiple transformations are dynamically applied to local training samples, inherently incorporating unlearning capabilities into the learning process itself. This means that the global model is forced to learn transformation-invariant features rather than memorizing sample-specific characteristics. However, training with extensively transformed data presents significant challenges, including potential distortion of task-relevant information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Qi (2022)</a></cite> that can lead to underfitting and convergence issues, as demonstrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning Framework ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. To mitigate this, we introduce a <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sample-dependent transformation strategy</span> to dynamically determine the optimal intensity level of transformation for each sample. Furthermore, we also propose a <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">transformation-invariant regularization technique</span> to encourage the model to exploit invariant features across all transformations of the same sample. The complete algorithm is outlined in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 4 Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning Framework ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, while types of transformations used in our experiments are provided in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="In Appendix C Implementation Details ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (Appendix).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample-dependent Transformation Strategy.</span> While applying multiple transformations helps reduce pattern memorization, excessive transformations can eliminate task-relevant information and degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al. (2020)</a></cite>. It is crucial to determine how much information is shared about the input, including both task-relevant and irrelevant information. In other words, we need to find the optimal intensity level of transformation that can be applied to per sample. Particularly, we introduce an inverse quantile value function (Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition6" title="Definition 6. ‣ 4 Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning Framework ‣ ToFU: Transforming How Federated Learning Systems Forget User Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) that dynamically determines the appropriate intensity level of transformation for each sample. This function leverages per-sample loss values calculated using the latest global model <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}^{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}^{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> during each round <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. It establishes an inverse relationship between sample loss and transformation intensity: samples with lower loss values (i.e., less irrelevant information) receive more transformations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we implement a progressive training approach by linearly increasing the maximum intensity level of transformation <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> throughout the training process <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\lceil\frac{t}{T}M\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\lceil\frac{t}{T}M\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of communication rounds and <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> represents the current round. For each training mini-batch, ToFU creates a transformed mini-batch with sample-specific transformation counts determined by relative loss values within the batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a vector of batch loss values.
The inverse quantile value function <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\digamma:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow[0,1]^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϝ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\digamma:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow[0,1]^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\digamma(\mathbf{x})=\left(\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}_{x_{j}&gt;x_{i}}}{n}\right)_{i=1}^{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\digamma(\mathbf{x})=\left(\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}_{x_{j}&gt;x_{i}}}{n}\right)_{i=1}^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟏</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the indicator function.
The number of applied transformations <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for sample <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is then determined by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\left\lceil M\cdot\digamma(\mathbf{x})_{i}\right\rceil," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ϝ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\left\lceil M\cdot\digamma(\mathbf{x})_{i}\right\rceil,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of allowed transformations.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformation-invariant Regularizer.</span> The transformation-invariant regularizer serves a critical function in our framework by compelling the model to identify and exploit correlated structures across multiple transformations rather than memorizing individual data points.
Particularly, beyond the standard supervised learning signal, we incorporate the KL divergence between the latent representations of transformed data <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{D}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathcal{T}(\mathcal{D}))</annotation></semantics></math> and that of original data <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transformation function. This regularization term maximizes mutual information between representations of transformed and original data, encouraging consistent feature extraction across different versions of the same sample. Formally, for each sample-label pair <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> in the client’s dataset, the loss function for progressive training is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\theta;x,y)=\underbrace{-y\log(p_{\theta}(x^{*}))}_{\text{Task loss}}+\gamma\underbrace{D_{KL}(f_{\theta}(x^{*})\parallel f_{\theta}(x))}_{\text{Transformation-invariant reg.}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Task loss</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Transformation-invariant reg.</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\theta;x,y)=\underbrace{-y\log(p_{\theta}(x^{*}))}_{\text{Task loss}}+\gamma\underbrace{D_{KL}(f_{\theta}(x^{*})\parallel f_{\theta}(x))}_{\text{Transformation-invariant reg.}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we use <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> unless stated otherwise.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>ToFU: Transformation-guided Federated Unlearning</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.6.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Input :</span><span id="algorithm1.6.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Number of clients </span><math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, batch size </span><math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, learning rate </span><math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, epochs </span><math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, max transformation level </span><math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Output :</span><span id="algorithm1.6.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Trained global model </span><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> with improved unlearning capabilities</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">1</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Server-side Coordination</span><span id="algorithm1.6.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">2</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Initialize global model </span><math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">3</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="algorithm1.6.5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><em id="algorithm1.6.5.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">communication round <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\ldots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\ldots,T</annotation></semantics></math></em><span id="algorithm1.6.5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.6" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">4</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="algorithm1.6.6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><em id="algorithm1.6.6.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">each client <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in parallel</em><span id="algorithm1.6.6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.7" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">5</span></span>
          <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}^{k}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">θ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}^{k}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.6.7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">LocalTraining(<em id="algorithm1.6.7.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></em>)</span><span id="algorithm1.6.7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.8" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm1.6.8.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
